1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,107
If your inline wheels click when you skate, you can usually find the cause in about five minutes at a table.

2
00:00:07,107 --> 00:00:10,727
Here is how to do it without replacing parts at random.

3
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:16,386
The cause is almost always a fit problem inside the wheel hub, not a worn out bearing.

4
00:00:16,386 --> 00:00:24,218
Most of the time the spacer does not match the wheel, a bearing is not pressed fully home, or an axle has worked loose.

5
00:00:24,218 --> 00:00:25,468
It is worth fixing.

6
00:00:25,468 --> 00:00:29,022
A wheel that clicks is moving somewhere it should not.

7
00:00:29,022 --> 00:00:32,641
Left alone, it wears the bearing seats in the hub oval.

8
00:00:32,641 --> 00:00:36,064
And a loose axle can eventually let go of the wheel.

9
00:00:36,764 --> 00:00:41,995
Skaters use words like click, knock, rattle and grind for very different problems.

10
00:00:41,995 --> 00:00:44,483
So start by splitting the sound in two.

11
00:00:44,483 --> 00:00:46,397
Lift the skate off the ground.

12
00:00:46,397 --> 00:00:49,013
Spin each wheel hard by hand, and listen.

13
00:00:49,013 --> 00:00:56,414
If you can still hear the click with no weight on the skate, it is attached to the wheel or sits inside the bearing.

14
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:01,582
If the click only shows up once you are standing on an edge, it is a fit problem.

15
00:01:01,582 --> 00:01:04,772
Something is moving under load that should not be.

16
00:01:04,772 --> 00:01:07,516
That one split saves most of the guesswork.

17
00:01:08,216 --> 00:01:10,776
Each sound points somewhere different.

18
00:01:10,776 --> 00:01:17,984
A sharp click once per wheel turn, even off the ground, means something is stuck to or broken in the wheel.

19
00:01:17,984 --> 00:01:25,529
A sharp click on each push, only under load, is usually a spacer mismatch or a bearing sitting proud of the hub.

20
00:01:25,529 --> 00:01:34,557
A dull knock on each push, which you often feel through the boot, points to frame mounting bolts or boot hardware that has gone slack.

21
00:01:34,557 --> 00:01:40,620
A loose rattle on rough surfaces is often a slack fastener or side play from a loose axle.

22
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:46,077
A dry grind or hiss that does not keep time with the wheel is inside the bearing.

23
00:01:46,077 --> 00:01:52,612
And a steady scuff on one wheel means it is touching the frame wall, or the wheel is badly coned.

24
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:59,655
If the click keeps time with the wheel rather than with your stride, you can often see the cause.

25
00:01:59,655 --> 00:02:04,887
Run a fingernail round the tread of each wheel and look closely at the urethane.

26
00:02:04,887 --> 00:02:12,211
A stone, a chip of glass or a grain of grit pressed into the surface will tick against the road once every turn.

27
00:02:12,211 --> 00:02:15,154
These collect easily on broken road surfaces.

28
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:18,489
Pick it out with a pin, and the noise goes with it.

29
00:02:18,489 --> 00:02:21,105
If the tread is clean, look at the core.

30
00:02:21,105 --> 00:02:27,841
A hairline crack in a plastic hub, or urethane starting to separate from the core, makes the same tick.

31
00:02:27,841 --> 00:02:29,410
Neither is worth saving.

32
00:02:29,410 --> 00:02:32,222
Take that wheel out of the set and move on.

33
00:02:32,922 --> 00:02:38,701
Most speed wheels hold two 608 bearings facing each other, with a spacer between them.

34
00:02:38,701 --> 00:02:40,246
The spacer has one job.

35
00:02:40,246 --> 00:02:43,942
It sets the exact distance between the two inner races.

36
00:02:43,942 --> 00:02:53,147
So when you tighten the axle, the clamping load runs through the spacer and the inner races, rather than squeezing the bearings together.

37
00:02:53,147 --> 00:02:58,859
Hubs, bearings and spacers are all made to tolerances, and those tolerances stack up.

38
00:02:58,859 --> 00:03:07,594
If you mix a new set of wheels with old spacers, or spacers from another brand, the stack can land a fraction of a millimetre out.

39
00:03:07,594 --> 00:03:10,214
That small gap is enough to make noise.

40
00:03:10,914 --> 00:03:14,228
This diagram shows three wheel hubs in cross section.

41
00:03:14,228 --> 00:03:18,793
The blue blocks are the bearings, and the bar between them is the spacer.

42
00:03:18,793 --> 00:03:20,982
On the left, the spacer is matched.

43
00:03:20,982 --> 00:03:25,109
The bearings sit flush with the hub faces, and the wheel is quiet.

44
00:03:25,109 --> 00:03:27,486
In the middle, the spacer is too long.

45
00:03:27,486 --> 00:03:30,487
It holds the bearings slightly proud of the hub.

46
00:03:30,487 --> 00:03:36,178
The frame clamps the bearings, but the wheel still has a sliver of room to shuffle on them.

47
00:03:36,178 --> 00:03:39,680
Every time you load an edge, the hub shifts and lets go.

48
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,993
That is the classic click under load.

49
00:03:41,993 --> 00:03:44,370
On the right, the spacer is too short.

50
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:48,309
Tightening the axle pulls the bearings inward and pinches them.

51
00:03:48,309 --> 00:03:49,810
You get drag, not noise.

52
00:03:50,510 --> 00:03:55,650
A long spacer and a bearing that will not seat feel the same through the skate.

53
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,537
But they have opposite fixes.

54
00:03:57,537 --> 00:03:59,228
So run this two step test.

55
00:03:59,228 --> 00:04:02,937
First, press both bearings home with the spacer left out.

56
00:04:02,937 --> 00:04:06,710
Run a fingernail across the hub face at each bearing edge.

57
00:04:06,710 --> 00:04:13,151
Then pull one bearing out, drop the spacer in, press both home again, and check the same two faces.

58
00:04:13,151 --> 00:04:18,681
Flush without the spacer but proud with it means the spacer is too long for that hub.

59
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:22,389
Proud both times means the bearing itself is not seating.

60
00:04:22,389 --> 00:04:29,156
Swap spacers between wheels and test again, because two wheels from the same set can behave differently.

61
00:04:29,156 --> 00:04:34,230
The quickest fix is often to go back to the spacers that came with your frame.

62
00:04:34,930 --> 00:04:38,135
The second cause looks identical from the outside.

63
00:04:38,135 --> 00:04:47,108
If a bearing went in at a slight angle, or stopped a hair short in a tight hub, the frame holds it but the hub does not support it properly.

64
00:04:47,108 --> 00:04:51,594
Under load it rocks in its seat, and you hear a click on every stride.

65
00:04:51,594 --> 00:04:57,170
With the spacer out, push each bearing straight in with your thumbs, keeping it square.

66
00:04:57,170 --> 00:05:01,849
It should seat with a firm, even push and finish level with the hub face.

67
00:05:01,849 --> 00:05:07,938
If one side always needs more force, look inside the hub for a burr or a trapped piece of grit.

68
00:05:07,938 --> 00:05:11,271
On well used wheels the bearing seats can wear oval.

69
00:05:11,271 --> 00:05:16,334
Once that happens, the click comes back however carefully you fit the bearings.

70
00:05:16,334 --> 00:05:18,193
That wheel has done its time.

71
00:05:18,893 --> 00:05:25,083
Hold the frame in one hand and try to rock the top of a wheel across the frame, not around it.

72
00:05:25,083 --> 00:05:33,775
If the wheel knocks from side to side, the axle is not firm, the spacer is missing, or the outer race has gone loose in a worn seat.

73
00:05:33,775 --> 00:05:38,187
Nip the axle up until the movement disappears, then spin the wheel.

74
00:05:38,187 --> 00:05:40,492
It should still run free and quiet.

75
00:05:40,492 --> 00:05:45,233
If tightening that far kills the spin, you are back to a spacer problem.

76
00:05:45,233 --> 00:05:49,842
Axles also work loose on their own, and Indian roads are hard on them.

77
00:05:49,842 --> 00:05:56,296
Watch for a noise that was not there at the start of a session and gets worse over the kilometres.

78
00:05:56,296 --> 00:06:01,037
Check your axles every few sessions, and always after a long road skate.

79
00:06:01,737 --> 00:06:05,413
Sometimes the sound is not coming from a wheel at all.

80
00:06:05,413 --> 00:06:10,655
This diagram marks four places to press and listen before you strip anything.

81
00:06:10,655 --> 00:06:13,310
Number one is the frame mounting bolts.

82
00:06:13,310 --> 00:06:17,667
A slightly slack bolt lets the frame move against the boot sole.

83
00:06:17,667 --> 00:06:21,547
That makes a dull knock you usually feel as well as hear.

84
00:06:21,547 --> 00:06:23,726
Number two is the heel hardware.

85
00:06:23,726 --> 00:06:27,470
It can loosen and tap, and it sounds just like a wheel.

86
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:29,580
Number three is the frame wall.

87
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:37,205
Look for a shiny scuff line, which usually means a coned wheel or a slightly bent frame leg after a kerb strike.

88
00:06:37,205 --> 00:06:39,792
Number four is the cuff and lace hook.

89
00:06:39,792 --> 00:06:42,992
A buckle slapping the boot is easy to rule out.

90
00:06:43,692 --> 00:06:46,551
Put it together, and the diagnosis is quick.

91
00:06:46,551 --> 00:06:51,750
Work on a table, change one thing at a time, and stop as soon as the noise goes.

92
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:59,613
Spin each wheel off the ground, then roll slowly, so you know whether the click needs load and which wheel it belongs to.

93
00:06:59,613 --> 00:07:01,823
Run a fingernail round that tread.

94
00:07:01,823 --> 00:07:10,271
Rock the wheel for side play, then put the key on every axle, mounting bolt and piece of boot hardware before you remove anything.

95
00:07:10,271 --> 00:07:14,755
If the click still needs load, pull the wheel and run the flush test.

96
00:07:14,755 --> 00:07:20,929
If it survives all that, move the same spacer and bearings into a wheel that has never clicked.

97
00:07:20,929 --> 00:07:24,178
If the noise follows them, the parts are at fault.

98
00:07:24,178 --> 00:07:27,168
If it stays with the old hub, the hub is worn.

99
00:07:27,867 --> 00:07:36,218
To keep it quiet, keep the spacers and axles that came with your frame together with that frame, and check your axles regularly.

100
00:07:36,218 --> 00:07:41,242
Clean bearings help too, because dirt hides a fit noise under a general hiss.

101
00:07:41,242 --> 00:07:46,591
And remember, new bearings only stop a click if the old bearings were the problem.

102
00:07:46,591 --> 00:07:48,875
With a click, they usually are not.

103
00:07:48,875 --> 00:07:51,615
So check the spacer and the seating first.

104
00:07:51,615 --> 00:07:57,356
The full guide on the LiveLica blog has the noise table, the fixes and common questions.

105
00:07:57,356 --> 00:07:59,378
The link is in the description.
